Greedy Algorithm
A Greedy Algorithm is an algorithm that solves a problem by choosing the best option available at each step, hoping to find the best overall solution.

& a greedy algorithm makes the best decision it can at the current moment without considering all possible future decisions.

Greedy only gives the best answer when a problem has two properties:
1.Greedy choice property: a locally optimal choice can always be extended to a globally optimal solution.
2.Optimal substructure: an optimal solution contains optimal solutions to its subproblems.

Classic problems where greedy is correct

1.Activity selection / interval scheduling: pick the activity that finishes earliest, then repeat.
2.Huffman coding: repeatedly merge the two least frequent symbols.
3.Dijkstra’s shortest path: (non-negative weights) always expand the closest unvisited node.
4.Kruskal’s and Prim’s algorithms: build a minimum spanning tree by adding the cheapest safe edge.
5.Fractional knapsack: take items in order of value-to-weight ratio.

Minimum Spanning Tree (MST)
A Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) is a tree that connects all vertices in a connected, weighted, undirected graph with the minimum possible total edge weight, without forming any cycles.
it must be
1.Connects all vertices (spanning),
2.Contains no cycles (tree, so exactly V − 1 edges), and
3.Has the smallest possible total edge weight.
